<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Führerprinzip</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Leader principle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Führerprinzip, or leader principle, was the basis of authority in Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It held that the leader's word stood above written law and that each official was responsible to his superior and owed him unconditional obedience, with all power flowing ultimately from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>. Applied to the party, the state and business, it replaced collective decision-making and elections.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fuhrerprinzip.jpg" alt="Führerprinzip" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nazi Party · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wochenspruch_der_NSDAP_16_February_1941.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The political-science term Führerprinzip was coined by Hermann von Keyserling, a Baltic German philosopher. As an ideology it sees an organisation as a hierarchy of leaders, each with absolute responsibility in his own area, owed absolute obedience and personal loyalty by those below and answerable to those above. Rudolf Hess described it as unconditional authority downwards and responsibility upwards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> proclaimed it the law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> as early as July 1921, when he left the party in a quarrel with its founder Anton Drexler and returned only when the committee accepted his demand to replace Drexler as chairman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> split into a northern faction led by Otto and Gregor Strasser, which disagreed about whether the principle was essential, and a southern faction led by Hitler. At the Bamberg Conference on 14 February 1926 Hitler defeated the opposition and made it the managing principle of the party. In 1934 he imposed it on the government and civil society, and organisations in schools, sports associations and factories were led by appointed leaders rather than elected committees. The armed forces swore a Führer Oath to Hitler personally from 1934. The legal theorist Carl Schmitt defended it as the foundation of the total state.</p><p>Propaganda supported the idea in films such as Der Herrscher and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolobrzeg">Kolberg</a>, and appealed to the memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>. Hermann Göring told the British ambassador that none of the leadership counted for more than the stones they stood on, and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrer">Führer</a> alone decided.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, defendants at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-trials">Nuremberg trials</a> of 1945 and 1946 and at the Eichmann trial in 1961 argued that the Führerprinzip voided their responsibility, since they were officers following superior orders. In Eichmann in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> (1963) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannah-arendt">Hannah Arendt</a> described Eichmann as an ordinary administrator who denied personal responsibility, the figure she called the banality of evil. Eichmann said he had been doing his job and had tried to act in accordance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a>'s categorical imperative.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Führerprinzip” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nazi Germany was a dictatorship built on the <b>Führerprinzip</b>, the principle that all authority flowed from Hitler, whose will stood above law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Führerprinzip</b> placed a single leader at the head of a hierarchy in which each member owed absolute obedience to those above him.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nazism">Nazism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
